# Line endings are LF in the working tree on every platform, not just in the repository.
#
# Without this, Git on Windows checks text files out as CRLF, and the golden-file tests compare
# freshly generated output (LF) against a committed fixture (CRLF) and fail - nine of them, in the
# parsers for CAMS, NSDL and the Zerodha tradebook. That is exactly how the v0.1.0 Windows release
# build died: it never reached the bundling step because `pnpm test` failed first. The CI frontend
# job runs on Linux only, so nothing caught it until a release was attempted.
#
# `eol=lf` rather than leaving it to each contributor's core.autocrlf, because a repository-level
# rule cannot be forgotten and does not depend on how anyone configured their machine.
#
# This also protects the CI check that rejects NUL bytes in source: that check reads files as bytes,
# and normalising them here keeps what is committed identical to what is tested.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Fixtures whose bytes are the point. A golden file is compared byte for byte, and a statement
# fixture is parsed as bytes; neither may be rewritten by an end-of-line rule.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
*.pdf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
